  "result": "I added `split(cents, parts)` to `shop/money.py` with tests, and the full suite passes: `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .` ran 11 tests, all OK. Nothing is committed.\n\n```python\ndef split(cents: int, parts: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Split cents into `parts` shares; the remainder goes one cent each to the first shares.\"\"\"\n    if parts < 1:\n        raise ValueError(f\"parts must be >= 1: {parts}\")\n    base, rem = divmod(cents, parts)\n    return [base + 1 if i < rem else base for i in range(parts)]\n```\n\nThere was no existing test file for the money module, so I created `tests/test_money.py` using `unittest`, like the other test files. It checks:\n- uneven splits, e.g. `split(10, 3) == [4, 3, 3]` and `split(11, 4) == [3, 3, 3, 2]`;\n- even splits and zero cents;\n- that the result always has `parts` elements summing to `cents`;\n- that `parts` of 0 or -1 raises `ValueError`.\n\nNegative amounts still sum correctly, but the extra cent rounds toward the larger-magnitude amounts, so the first share isn't the bigger one. For example, `split(-5, 3)` returns `[-1, -2, -2]`, not `[-2, -2, -1]`. Your spec didn't cover negative amounts, so I left this as is. If refunds or negative balances might go through `split`, tell me which ordering you want and I'll lock it in with a test.",
